+++
!!Apamea!! (Greek: Ἀπάμεια, //Apameia//; Arabic: أفامية, [[ romanized | romanization-of-arabic ]]: //afāmiyah//) began as a [[ Macedonian | ancient-macedonians ]] military settlement called !!Pella!!, planted on the right bank of the [[ Orontes River ]] in what is now northwest [[ Syria ]] in the aftermath of the [[ Wars of the Diadochi ]]. Around 300 BC [[ Seleucus I Nicator ]] fortified it, made it a city and renamed it after his wife, [[ Apama ]]. It was the capital of [[ Apamene ]] under the [[ Seleucids | seleucid-empire ]][[ ^1 ]] and later of the [[ Roman province ]] of [[ Syria Secunda | roman-syria ]], a rank it kept into the [[ Crusader period | crusader-states ]]. What stands today is an ancient [[ Hellenistic | hellenistic-period ]] and [[ Roman | ancient-rome ]] city.

Rome arrived in 64 BC, when [[ Pompey ]] razed the fortress. The city later held out through a three-year siege during the revolt of [[ Quintus Caecilius Bassus ]]. The [[ Antioch earthquake | 115-antioch-earthquake ]] of 115 AD wrecked it, but rebuilding began soon afterwards and major reconstruction went on through the second century. [[ Khosrow I ]] destroyed it again in the sixth century, and [[ Sasanian | sasanian-empire ]] forces took it during the [[ Byzantine–Sasanian War | byzantinesasanian-war-of-572591 ]] before it was retaken. After the [[ Muslim conquest of Syria ]] Apamea was partially resettled and rose again under the [[ Hamdanid dynasty ]]; it later passed to Crusader control under Tancred, and an earthquake in 1152 destroyed it.

Two structures dominate the ruins. The Great Colonnade, an avenue of columns roughly 2 km (1.2 mi) long, was one of the longest such structures in the [[ Roman world | roman-empire ]]; rebuilt after the 115 AD earthquake and later modified under the [[ Byzantine | byzantine-empire ]] emperor [[ Justinian I ]], it formed the city's [[ cardo maximus | cardo ]] and connected [[ baths | thermae ]], an [[ agora ]] and a [[ nymphaeum ]]. The Roman Theatre, with an estimated [[ seating capacity ]] exceeding 20,000, is among the largest surviving [[ theatres of the Roman Empire | roman-theatre-structure ]]. There is also a late antique [[ synagogue ]] with an inscribed mosaic floor, later succeeded on the same site by a Christian [[ church | church-architecture ]], and the Great Hunting Mosaic, a large early 5th-century floor [[ mosaic ]] recovered from a building believed to have been the residence of the provincial governor.

Much of what was found here is no longer here. Numerous artifacts, among them a reconstructed section of the Great Colonnade and the Great Hunting Mosaic, are held at the [[ Art & History Museum ]] in [[ Brussels ]]. Since the onset of the [[ Syrian civil war ]], the site itself has been damaged by looting.

== Geography

The site lies on the edge of the modern town of [[ Qalaat al-Madiq ]], about 55 km (34 mi) to the northwest of [[ Hama ]], [[ Syria ]], looking out over the [[ Ghab valley ]].

== History

Apamea was founded as a Macedonian military settlement, passed to Rome in 64 BC, and was destroyed by an earthquake in 1152. The eras in between are taken in turn below.

== Hellenistic era

Who founded Pella, and when, is argued over. A mosaic of Apamea discovered in 2011, read by Olszewski and Saad as a new historical and iconographic source for Hellenistic history,[[ ^2 ]] puts the foundation of the Macedonian military camp (//katoikia//) in the fall 320 BC, just after the Treaty of Triparadeisos (320 BC), at the initiative of [[ Antipater ]] and with [[ Cassander ]]'s inspiration — this in the years after [[ Alexander the Great ]] conquered the region and his generals went to war over it. In view of that interpretation, the authors disagree with the earlier hypotheses attributing the foundation of Pella to Alexander the Great or to [[ Antigonus I Monophthalmus ]]. From about 300 BC Pella received the new status of //polis//: it was fortified and established as a city by [[ Seleucus I Nicator ]], who named it after his [[ Bactrian | bactria ]] wife, [[ Apama ]], daughter of the [[ Sogdian | sogdia ]] warlord [[ Spitamenes ]].[[ ^3 ]]

The site sat in a loop of the Orontes which, with the lake and marshes, gave it a peninsular form — hence its other name, Cherronêsos. It stood at a strategic crossroads for Eastern commerce and became one of the four cities of the [[ Syrian tetrapolis ]]. Seleucus also made it a military base with 500 elephants, and an equestrian stud with 30,000 mares and 300 stallions.

After 142 BC, the pretender [[ Diodotus Tryphon ]] made Apamea the base of his operations.[[ ^4 ]]

In AD 6, Q. Aemilius Secundus[[ ^5 ]] surveyed the population of the city and the territory that belonged to it, and counted "117,000 hom(ines) civ(ium)" – 117,000 citizen human beings. What that figure means for the total population depends on the method used: it has been interpreted as 130,000 or as 500,000.[[ ^6 ]]

== Early Roman period

In 64 BC [[ Pompey ]] marched south from his winter quarters, probably at or near [[ Antioch ]], and razed the fortress of Apamea as the city was annexed to the [[ Roman Republic ]].[[ ^7 ]] When Syria revolted under [[ Quintus Caecilius Bassus ]], the city held out against [[ Julius Caesar ]] for three years, until [[ Cassius | gaius-cassius-longinus ]] arrived in 46 BC.[[ ^8 ]] Apamea was briefly [[ captured in 40 BC | pompeianparthian-invasion-of-40-bc ]] by the Pompeian-Parthian forces. On the outbreak of the [[ Jewish War | first-jewish-roman-war ]], the inhabitants of Apamea spared the Jews who lived in their midst and would not suffer them to be murdered or led into captivity.[[ ^9 ]][[ ^10 ]]

Much of Apamea was destroyed in the [[ 115 AD earthquake | 115-antioch-earthquake ]], but it was subsequently rebuilt.

== Late Roman and Byzantine eras

From 218 until 234 AD the legion [[ II Parthica | legio-ii-parthica ]] was stationed in Apamea. It was here that the legion abandoned the usurper [[ Macrinus ]] and sided with [[ Elagabalus ]]' rise to the purple; Elagabalus then defeated Macrinus in the Battle of Antioch.[[ ^11 ]]

The [[ Targum ]] of Pseudo-Jonathan ([[ Num. | book-of-numbers ]] xxxiv. 11) has Apamea (אפמיאה)[[ ^12 ]] for the city name Shepham, as it occurs in the [[ Targumim | targum ]] [[ Jerusalem ]] and [[ Neofiti ]].[[ ^13 ]] Since Apamea virtually belonged to Rabbinic Palestine, the first-fruits that [[ Ariston | ariston-of-apamea ]] brought from the town were accepted for sacrifice in Jerusalem.[[ ^14 ]]

In the early 5th century Apamea was granted the status of provincial capital of [[ Syria Secunda ]], after that province's separation from Syria Prima.[[ ^15 ]]

The city was [[ destroyed by Khosrow I | battle-of-apamea-573 ]] in the 6th century.[[ ^16 ]][[ ^17 ]] During the [[ Byzantine–Sasanian War of 602–628 ]] it fell in 613 to [[ Shahrbaraz ]] and stayed in Sasanian hands until near the end of the war.[[ ^18 ]]

== Islamic era

After the [[ Muslim conquest of Syria ]] Apamea was partially rebuilt, and became known in Arabic as Afāmiya or Fāmiya.[[ ^16 ]][[ ^17 ]] The [[ Rashidun ]] conquered it, together with the city of [[ Al-Suqaylabiyah ]], after the [[ battle of the Yarmuk ]].[[ ^19 ]] Apamea was then used as an observation post to warn against enemy attack.[[ ^19 ]]

The Arab tribes of [[ Bahra ]] and [[ Udhra | banu-udhra ]] settled it, but it regained its importance only under the rule of the Aleppo-based [[ Hamdanid dynasty ]].[[ ^17 ]] [[ Khalaf ibn Mula'ib ]] ruled the city from 1095–1106; after Khalaf's murder by the [[ Assassins | order-of-assassins ]] it was taken by [[ Tancred | tancred-prince-of-galilee ]].[[ ^20 ]] An earthquake destroyed it in 1152.[[ ^16 ]]

== Remains

Many remains of the ancient acropolis are still standing — probably the remains of the highly decorated temples of which Sozomen speaks.[[ ^21 ]] The acropolis is now enclosed in the ancient castle walls called [[ Kalat el-Mudik | qalaat-al-madiq ]] (Kŭlat el-Mudîk); the remainder of the ancient city is to be found in the plain.

The most significant collection of objects from the site that can be seen outside of Syria, including many significant architectural and artistic objects, is in Brussels at the [[ Cinquantenaire Museum ]].

The [[ civil war in Syria | syrian-civil-war ]] has brought damage and looting by treasure hunters.[[ ^22 ]][[ ^23 ]] In April 2017 //[[ Al-Masdar News ]]// published satellite photographs revealing the site covered in hundreds of holes dug by treasure hunters seeking ancient artifacts.[[ ^24 ]]

== Great Colonnade

The Great Colonnade ran along the main avenue of Apamea for nearly 2 kilometres (1.2 mi), which put it among the longest in the [[ Roman world | roman-empire ]]. The original dated from the Seleucid Empire and was devastated along with the rest of Apamea in the [[ 115 AD earthquake | 115-antioch-earthquake ]]. Reconstruction started immediately, and over the course of the second century the city was completely rebuilt, starting with the Great Colonnade.[[ ^25 ]] The colonnade was aligned along the north-south axis, making up the city's "[[ cardo maximus ]]", and ran in an uninterrupted straight line from the north gate to the south gate. A monumental votive column that stood opposite the baths marks the northern third of its stretch.[[ ^26 ]] The colonnade passed through the centre of the city, and several important buildings were clustered around it: the baths, the [[ agora ]], the Temple of [[ Tyche ]], the [[ nymphaeum ]], the [[ rotunda | rotunda-architecture ]], the [[ atrium | atrium-architecture ]] church and the basilica.[[ ^27 ]]

On either side of the street a 6.15 metres (20.2 ft)-wide colonnade ran its full length. The columns were 9 metres (30 ft) high and 0.9 metres (2 ft 11 in) in diameter, and stood on square bases of 1.24 m on a side and 0.47 m high. They display two main designs, plain and distinctive [[ spiral flutes | fluting-architecture ]]; archaeologist Jean Lassus argues that the plain columns date back to the [[ Trajanic | trajan ]] period and the fluted ones to that of [[ Antoninus Pius ]].[[ ^28 ]] The porticoes were paved with extensive mosaics along the full stretch of the colonnade.[[ ^26 ]]

Under the [[ Byzantine | byzantine-empire ]] Emperor [[ Justinian I ]], several parts of the colonnade were restored. The street was narrowed to 12 m by adding a walkway on either side, and several stretches had their Roman pavement replaced with a new pavement of squared blocks of limestone, which also covered a completely overhauled drainage system. Justinian's changes included erecting a monumental //[[ tetrastylon | portico#tetrastyle ]]// made up of four 9 m high columns with metre-high [[ capitals | capital-architecture ]].[[ ^29 ]] The city was, however, later sacked by the [[ Sasanians ]] under [[ Adarmahan ]].[[ ^30 ]]

A reconstructed section of the colonnade can be seen in the Brussels [[ Cinquantenaire Museum ]].

== Roman theatre

The theatre was originally built in [[ Hellenistic | hellenistic-civilization ]] style in the early [[ Seleucid Empire ]], then expanded and remodelled in the early [[ Roman period | roman-syria ]],[[ ^31 ]] when the main stage and entrances were reorganized in a more typical Roman fashion. The [[ 115 Antioch earthquake ]] caused severe damage; it was rebuilt soon afterwards under the patronage of both [[ Trajan ]] and [[ Hadrian ]], and expanded further in the first half of the third century CE.[[ ^32 ]] Under the [[ Byzantine Empire ]] the theatre's [[ drainage basin ]] was restructured and a //[[ qanat ]]// was built through the middle of the lower stage. By the late Byzantine period the theatre had stopped serving as a centre for theatrical performances, but the theatre and its //qanat// continued to be an important water source during the Byzantine and Islamic periods.[[ ^33 ]] It was built into a steep hill overlooking the [[ Orontes River ]] valley.[[ ^34 ]]

With an estimated seating capacity in excess of 20,000, the theatre is, along with the one at [[ Ephesus ]], one of the largest surviving theatres of the [[ Roman Empire ]]; the only other known theatre that is considerably larger was the [[ Theatre of Pompey ]] in [[ Rome ]].[[ ^35 ]] Much of its structure is in ruins due to architectural collapses and extensive quarrying in later epochs,[[ ^36 ]] and only one-eighth of the site has been exposed so far.[[ ^35 ]] One of its main features is the water basin and the elaborate Roman piping system used in it; the recently excavated [[ terracotta ]] system, located along the eastern ground entrance, is well preserved.[[ ^37 ]]

== Great hunting mosaic

This mosaic, now in the [[ Cinquantenaire Museum ]], Brussels, was discovered in 1935 in the reception room of what was probably the palace of the Roman governor of the province of [[ Syria Secunda ]]. Its area is 120 m^^2^^.

The great mosaic dates from 415–420 AD and is amongst the most prestigious of this type of composition. Technically and thematically it is comparable with mosaics of the same period in the Palace of the [[ Byzantine emperors | byzantine-emperor ]] in Constantinople.

An inscription at the entrance states: "During the most beautiful Apellion, the triclinium was rebuilt in the month Gorpiaios, third indict, in the year 851" (September, 539 AD).

== Synagogue

Near the city's center, a 4th-century Jewish synagogue was discovered. Its geometric mosaic floor dates from around 391 CE and carries 19 inscriptions recording the names of the donors, as well as their offices, including '[[ archisynagogos | archisynagogue ]]' and '[[ hazzan ]]' or 'diakonos.' In the 5th century, a Christian church was built on the site of the synagogue.[[ ^38 ]]

== Bishopric

Christianity came to the area within the first century. Bishops included:

- Marcellus of Apamea (fl. 375)
- [[ Alexander of Apamea ]] (fl. 431)
- Alpheius of Apamea Asistente al consilio de Neocesarea
- Julian of Apamea, saint and opponent of Montanism (S. III)
- Domnus (fl. [[ 451 | council-of-chalcedon ]])
- Epiphanius (451/457–after 471)[[ ^39 ]]
- Isaac (ordained bef. 512, died c. 513/514)[[ ^40 ]]
- Stephen (in 512–516)[[ ^40 ]]
- Cosmas (in 512–516)[[ ^40 ]]
- [[ Peter of Apamea ]], monophysite[[ ^41 ]] (515/516–518/519)

Today a number of Christian denominations maintain [[ titular sees | titular-see ]] for the town. These include

- [[ Latin Catholic titular Metropolitan archbishopric | roman-catholic-archdiocese-of-apamea ]]
- Melkite Catholic titular Metropolitan archbishopric
- Syriac Catholic Catholic titular Metropolitan archbishopric
- Maronite Catholic titular bishopric

== People

- [[ Al-Muqtana ]] – (11th-century [[ Ismāʿīlī ]][[ Governor ]] and a founder of the [[ Druze Faith ]], the primary exponent of the [[ Divine call ]] and author of several of the [[ Epistles of Wisdom ]]) [[ ^42 ]][[ ^43 ]]
- [[ Marinus | marinus-praetorian-prefect ]] – (praetorian prefect)
- [[ Archigenes ]] – (physician)
- [[ Diodotus Tryphon ]] – (official under King [[ Alexander I Balas ]])
- [[ Aristarchus of Thessalonica ]] – (bishop, one of the [[ Seventy Apostles | seventy-disciples ]])
- [[ Evagrius Scholasticus ]] – (6th-century historian)
- [[ Iamblichus of Chalcis ]] – ([[ Neo-Platonist ]] philosopher)
- [[ Sopater of Apamea ]] – (sophist and Neoplatonist philosopher)
- [[ Alexander of Apamea ]] – 5th-century bishop
- [[ Alcibiades of Apamea ]]
- [[ Junias ]] – (1st-century bishop)
- [[ Numenius of Apamea ]] – (2nd century philosopher)
- Polychronius – (bishop, and brother of [[ Theodore of Mopsuestia ]])
- [[ Posidonius ]] – (Greek philosopher and author, 2nd–1st Century BCE)
- [[ Eunus ]] – rebel [[ slave | slavery-in-ancient-rome ]] and self proclaimed King of [[ Enna ]], led the [[ First Servile War ]] against the [[ Roman Republic ]]
- [[ Pseudo-Oppian ]] poet
- [[ Sextus Varius Marcellus ]] – (3rd-century [[ Roman Equestrian | roman-equestrian-order ]] and later governor of [[ Numidia ]]. Husband of [[ Julia Soaemias ]] and father of Roman emperor [[ Elagabalus ]])
- [[ Theodoret ]] – (5th-century bishop)

